[[:db/cas 17592186297248 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297250 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297250 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297251 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297256 :question/problem "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},\n$$ then what is $Y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186297256 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} &= \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 \\\\ &= \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} &= \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 \\\\ &= \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297278 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297283 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297286 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186297287 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297296 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297297 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x>-1.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\nfor $x>-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186297298 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants."] [:db/cas 17592186297312 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297313 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297314 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297315 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297327 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=5.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=5.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297341 :question/problem "$\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297342 :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297344 :question/problem "$\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297347 :question/problem "$\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297358 :question/problem "$\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297372 :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." "For the curve $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,\n$$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186297372 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$" "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},\n$$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186297379 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297381 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297382 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297384 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297385 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297386 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297389 :question/problem "$\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297390 :question/problem "$\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297395 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297395 :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)" "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186297396 :question/problem "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297397 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297400 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297407 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$"] [:db/cas 17592186297410 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297411 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297427 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186297428 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n ${\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}($\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186297432 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186297434 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasi