}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186302229 :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,$\n$y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1\n$$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,$\n$y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$"] [:db/cas 17592186302246 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186302262 :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle ]⋃(\n$$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186302263 :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle ]⋃[\n$$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186302266 :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186302267 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302268 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302279 :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186302308 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302318 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,-2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only point where the slope is **NOT** evaluated is $(\\frac{3}{2},-2).$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,-2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only point where the slope is **NOT** evaluated is $(\\frac{3}{2},-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186302319 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302324 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}