aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(-\\frac{2}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305138 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-10{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(20\\beta 